/usr/bin/kwic from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for kwic, so this is the disassembly, and kwic.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f kwic.ctl kwic.dist

kwic.dist is the binary off inode 54 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 4671
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header - see
../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 1075 and c.ents
at 0f28.  The program is the one of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's
Software Tools, and the manual page is the one that book's index is made with:

    kwic <text | sort | unrot >index

Its own code is three functions and there is no string in it.  main at 010e
reads lines with getl - 0442, which is the library's, handed 512 - and gives
each to kwic at 0168, until a read comes back with nothing.  kwic turns every
tab in the line into a blank, and then goes along it: where there is a letter
or a digit it calls rotate at 025f with the line and the place, and goes past
the rest of the run of them.  rotate writes from that place to the end of the
line, or to the newline, then a dollar sign, then the line from its start up to
the place, then a newline.  So a word is a run of letters and digits, as
tested by the comparisons with a, z, A, Z, 0 and 9 and nothing else, and the
blank before it comes round to the end of the line with the rest.

THE THINGS IN IT THAT ARE NOT WHAT THE MANUAL SAYS

A line of more than 511 characters comes back from getl in pieces of 512, a
newline included, and each piece is a line.  A word that is cut in two by the
join is two words, and a piece with no newline is rotated to the end of it.

The output has no byte with the high bit set in it.  The characters are passed
to putc as chars, widened to ints, and the library's putc of a negative number
is the request to flush the stream, so the byte is not written - 05d2 tests the
sign of the high byte and goes round the store.  The text is expected to be
ASCII.  A word with an accented letter in it loses that letter from the output
and is split at it, since the letter is not alphanumeric either.  kwic.c does
the same, in out(), and says why.

The exit status is whatever BC held when the input ended, which was the count
getl returned, and that was 0, so it is the one place where it comes out right.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte: ordinary text, a few
hundred lines of it, with tabs; text with words of every kind, punctuation,
digits, underscores and a letter with an accent; lines of 512, 511 and
more than 1500 characters; a line with no newline; blank lines; an empty file;
and four thousand random bytes with newlines, carriage returns, NULs and bytes
over 0177.
